Acceptance criteria:
The actual native lower framebuffer draws at each clicked LCD pixel, not merely
somewhere on the screen. Physical UVs retain their original mapping in any
visible orientation. Mouse/touch coordinates remain accurate across zoom,
resizing and device-pixel ratios. Upper LCDs, hidden/covered/back-facing surfaces
and points outside the LCD do not become touchscreen targets. GBA behavior,
unrelated DS ROM bytes, pose locks and save formats remain unchanged.
Ordinary short mouse/touch taps work without artificially holding contact.
Queued drag moves do not build a stale backlog; cancellation discards pending
contacts rather than replaying them after blur, pause, tools or console changes.

Completion evidence:
The software denial fixture now retains its CDP session; detaching restores
prompt, as an isolated probe proves. Native GBA manual restoration passes with
denial asserted. DS also boots under denial and records all four quick-tap
native marks yellow, but every copied lower-LCD target remains green. Copying
was gated by expensive room paints. With decoupled copying, all four native
and copied targets are yellow, but the evaluation itself takes5093ms and
misses1500ms. The flat precision case passes two native/physical targets and
the third native mark, then exhausts120s in a pointer-state evaluation.
Trivial evaluations take4-10s; responsiveness remains unqualified while
actual rendering stalls are investigated. The original compound case exhausts120s at
its second corner. Four independent locked-pose cases retain all twelve
targets, actual held mouse/touch drags and native/physical checks; reuse the
successful native snapshot for each brush assertion. No deadlines changed.
The subsequent GPU-backpressure correction passes flat-corner precision
but quick taps still exceed1500ms: correct native/copied pixels arrive
